<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Faerie Court Wars

''YOLD ~3195 – ~4200.'' The clan wars of the settlement age — a thousand years in which the fae
clans of Elyncia fought over a world that had just changed shape, and out of which the
''Neo-Thracian Empire'' rose as the thing that won.

''The Wars stand distinct from the War of the Immortals.'' That older conflict — YOLD 1, a decade
of strife among gods and immortals, ending in the First Breaking — belongs to the elder age and
its cast. The Court Wars run two thousand years later, among clans, over ground.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-bracket>>

!! Why the Era Sits Where It Sits

''The lower bound: the Apophis Datum, YOLD 3195.'' The Datum re-based every coast, delta, harbor,
and reef-line on the planet. The clans that spread inland afterward followed rivers and shorelines
''the Datum had just built'' — which is why human settlement of the interior valleys dates to
~3300–3500 and not earlier. ''You cannot settle a coast that does not exist yet.''

''The upper bound: Neo-Thracia consolidates.'' By ~4200 the Empire stands as a political power in
its own right, opening the eight-century golden age that runs to the Fall at YOLD 5000. The Wars
belong to the era ''before'' that consolidation — the fighting that produced it.

''So the Wars and the settlement name one event.'' The clans spread across new geography; the
clans already holding that ground disputed it; the argument ran a thousand years and ended with an
empire. ''What the Court Wars settled was who got the world the Datum made.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-combatants>>

!! Who Fought

''Fae against fae, throughout'' — humanity counting as one of the clans
(`lar:///ha.ka.ba/elyncia/concepts/the-fae-clans`). No inter-species war ever happened on Elyncia,
because the category was never available.

What ran through the Wars instead: the ''human clan's ascendancy'', pressed under the conceit that
it stood as the best of the clans; older clans defending holdings that predated the newcomers by
three thousand years; Courts gathering, breaking, and re-gathering as allegiances shifted faster
than descent could follow.

''The war left its metal behind.'' Powered armor, personal daemon-cores, and the sacral-military
kit of the losing clans survive as relics — found, inherited, and re-used for millennia afterward.
Ichi's armor stands as one such piece: made in these Wars, recovered and carried down three
generations by a clan native to the eastern shelf of Mu — the ground that became the Broken Isles —
and turned against Neo-Thracian invaders twenty centuries after the war that forged it ended.
//(Full line: [[ichi|lar:///ha.ka.ba/elyncia/characters/primary-characters/ichi]].)//

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-era-ladder>>

!! Design Law ~ the Era Ladder

''Tech and Arcane levels re-set as the timeline advances.'' Elyncia's history runs long enough,
and its Breakings cut deep enough, that ''no single technological register governs the whole
span''. Each era carries its own ladder rung — and the Court Wars and the imperial age that
follows them stand as ''deliberately open ground'' for drawing pattern-integrities in from other
games and fictions.

''What this permits.'' An era may take a whole cultural-technological vector whole, so long as the
vector reads as ''integral'' — a pattern with its own internal logic, not a decoration. The
timeline supplies the slot; the borrowed pattern supplies the texture; the Breakings supply the
reason the register changed.

''The ladder's sibling.'' Where an era absorbs a pattern as ''texture'', the Loom delivers other
worlds' patterns as ''objects and places'' — wrack on the shore, relics out of the flux-lands,
dream realms in the noosphere. Same discipline, different door:
[[the-reachable-worlds|lar:///ha.ka.ba/elyncia/concepts/the-reachable-worlds]].

''Worked example — Neo-Thracia at its Fall (YOLD 5000).'' The Empire at its height had acquired:

* ''Jakub Różalski 1920+ dieselpunk'' — walking war-machines over agrarian country; peasantry and
  towering mechanized force sharing one horizon; the mundane and the enormous in a single frame.
* ''Late-Roman Thracian cavalry mythos'' — the soldier-emperor pattern, where the cavalry arm
  becomes the decisive force and then becomes the throne; provincial horsemen taking an empire that
  no longer knows how to refuse them.

''The hint already stands in the Caverns.'' The garden-palace's wonders — the false sun, the
teleport globes, the escape pods, the boiler-works — never read as anachronism. They read as
''imperial magitech'', because at its height that is exactly the register the Empire ran at.

//(Referee note: the ladder cuts both ways. An era's register also tells you what a ruin from that
era should feel like when the party walks into it five hundred years later.)//

!!! The Middle Stays Loose ~ ruled

''The Court Wars carry no named internal phases, and shall not acquire them.'' A thousand-year span
of cascading wars resists periodization by design: the era stays ''deliberately loose'' so that any
pattern-integrity drawn into it can find its own depth, rather than being told in advance which rung
it lands on.

''Tech and Arcane levels fluctuated wildly across the whole planet through these wars.'' Not one
ladder rung for the era — ''many, simultaneously, and unevenly distributed''. A front running
high magitech and a front running bronze and oath could stand a week's sail apart, and often did.
Local registers rose, collapsed, and rose again as ley-access, patronage, and planar reach shifted
under the fighting.

''What this gives a referee:'' the middle era answers "what technology do they have here" with
''whatever this place's war left it'' — and answers it differently for every place. A ruin from the
Court Wars carries no default register; it carries the register of the front it belonged to.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/open>>

!! Open

* ''What the clans did for the three thousand years between'' the First Breaking and the Wars —
  the long coexistence, whatever its character, that the fighting finally ended.
* ''Which Courts stood on which side'', and whether the modern Court roster took its present shape
  during the Wars or after them.
* ''Which Nexuses ran which register when'' — the flux stands canon, its map does not.

<<~/ahu>>

